    14	# Design analysis for the combo variant
    15	# =====================================
    16	# concretization_and_trace:
    17	# - The input still has four sections split by blue (1) full rows/columns in the
    18	#   order template / stencil / fg swatch / bg swatch.
    19	# - The fusion changes section 2 in two linked ways. We no longer read green pixels
    20	#   with 4-connectivity; we read 8-connected green groups. Also, the width of each
    21	#   internal separator in the output is the pixel area of the corresponding ordered
    22	#   8-connected group.
    23	# - A concrete stencil example for a horizontal layout is:
    24	#       110  110  110
    25	#       001  000  001
    26	#            011  001
    27	#   with black gaps between the three clusters. Interpreting each cluster:
    28	#   first area = 3, second area = 4, third area = 4. Under the source task, these
    29	#   are six 4-connected mini-pieces, so the output would repeat the tile six times
    30	#   with 1-cell separators. Under this combo, they become three 8-connected groups,
    31	#   so the output has three copies; the first separator is 3 cells wide and the
    32	#   second separator is 4 cells wide. The last group's area is still visible in the
    33	#   stencil and still contributes to the repeat count, but there is no separator
    34	#   after the final copy because only internal gaps are drawn.
    35	#
    36	# complexity_calibration:
    37	# - The generator uses exactly two area families, 3 and 4, built from diagonal-only
    38	#   joins. This keeps the rule easy to observe: humans can count green pixels inside
    39	#   each diagonal cluster and compare them against separator widths.
    40	# - Freedom is intentionally limited. Every sample contains three ordered 8-connected
    41	#   groups, and the first two visible separators are always widths 3 and 4 in some
    42	#   order. That repeated support makes the fused rule inferable from few examples.
    43	# - The cluster shapes differ only in height, not width, so input size stays compact
    44	#   while the relevant area change remains visually legible.
    45	#
    46	# implicit_assumption_audit_for_main:
    47	# - `_split_sections` assumes only the true separators are full blue rows/columns.
    48	#   The generator never uses color 1 inside any section.
    49	# - `template_mask = template != 0` assumes section 1 contains only black background
    50	#   plus one non-black template shape. The generator preserves that.
    51	# - `bbox(template_mask)` assumes the template is non-empty. `random_sprite` provides
    52	#   a non-empty connected shape, so the bbox always exists in generated samples.
    53	# - `_ordered_components` assumes the intended stencil objects are green (3) and are
    54	#   spatially ordered along the repeat axis without ties. The generator places each
    55	#   cluster in a left-to-right or top-to-bottom sequence with fixed blank gaps.
    56	# - `_repeat_with_component_gaps` assumes the gap widths come from the first N-1
    57	#   ordered 8-connected components. This is the only non-symmetric part of the rule;
    58	#   the final component still affects the copy count but cannot create an external
    59	#   trailing gap because the output only contains separators between adjacent copies.
    60	#
    61	# placement_legality:
    62	# - Each stencil cluster is a small boolean mask placed wholly inside the stencil.
    63	# - Consecutive clusters are separated by two blank columns/rows along the layout
    64	#   axis, so no accidental 8-neighbor merge can occur across different components.
    65	# - Cluster heights/widths are at most 3, while the shared axis is `crop_dim + 2`
    66	#   with `crop_dim` in 2..3, so every placement fits comfortably.
    67	# - The generator rejects any sample whose final input or output would exceed a
    68	#   30x30 grid, keeping the construction inside ordinary ARC size limits.
    69	#
    70	# observability_and_cleanliness:
    71	# - The stencil is clean: black background plus three green diagonal clusters with
    72	#   obvious empty gaps between them. Nothing overlaps, and each cluster can be
    73	#   counted both by group count and by pixel area with the naked eye.
    74	# - The output is equally clean because only one template crop is repeated, and the
    75	#   only varying feature is the thickness of the background-colored separator bands.
    76	# - Because the visible separators are always one width-3 band and one width-4 band,
    77	#   the area-to-gap correspondence has repeated support instead of appearing only as
